From 7f2a2473ce1841ad5fd9edf31f45b049af5bf907 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Kubec <kubec@openssl.foundation>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:57:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] TLS1.3: Ignore a CertificateRequest received after
 close_notify

A TLSv1.3 client with post-handshake authentication enabled is meant to ignore
a CertificateRequest that arrives after it has sent close_notify. The read
transition had already switched the PHA state to SSL_PHA_REQUESTED by the time
tls_process_certificate_request() decided to ignore the message, so the write
transition for TLS_ST_CR_CERT_REQ never reached its "after close_notify" branch
and moved on to TLS_ST_CW_CERT instead. The client then wrote Certificate and
Finished after its own close_notify, and did so without calling
tls_prepare_client_certificate(), which means the application's cert_cb was not
consulted.

- RFC 8446 6.1: The client and the server must share knowledge that the
  connection is ending in order to avoid a truncation attack. This alert
  notifies the recipient that the sender will not send any more messages on this
  connection.  Any data received after a closure alert has been received MUST be
  ignored.

Discovered by Anthropic's OSS Scanner (finding ANT-2026-AOH3FCZ3).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-dable-5-2
Reviewed-by: Tomas Mraz <tomas@openssl.foundation>
Reviewed-by: Frederik Wedel-Heinen <fwh.openssl@gmail.com>
Merge-date: Mon Nov 23 17:09:28 2026
Merged-from: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/33723
(cherry picked from commit 49442b4e3572367cd5b94e4106e6f7030be7b680)
---
 ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c b/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
index bdf99cd..9080458 100644
--- a/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
+++ b/ssl/statem/statem_clnt.c
@@ -2620,8 +2620,11 @@ MSG_PROCESS_RETURN tls_process_certificate_request(SSL_CONNECTION *s,
             /*
              * We already sent close_notify. This can only happen in TLSv1.3
              * post-handshake messages. We can't reasonably respond to this, so
-             * we just ignore it
+             * we just ignore it. The read transition already moved us to
+             * SSL_PHA_REQUESTED; undo that so that the write transition does
+             * not go on to send a Certificate.
              */
+            s->post_handshake_auth = SSL_PHA_EXT_SENT;
             return MSG_PROCESS_FINISHED_READING;
         }
 
